# Stage 3 of three. 'AeroAssembly.assy' is what 'pc import assembly' read out of
# 'AeroAssembly.step'; 'AeroAssembly_corrected.assy' is that record with its
# three defects taken out, still written as eight coordinates; this file is the
# same eight parts with no coordinate in it at all.
#
# Every item but the first says what holds it, what it is held by, and - in
# 'how:' - what somebody has to do to make that joint. The plate is placed by
# being first, which is the one placement an assembly that is going to be built
# is allowed to make (see 'partcad.test.connectivity').
#
# The geometry this produces is the geometry 'AeroAssembly_corrected.assy'
# produces. The ports below sit on the very faces and hole axes those corrected
# placements put face to face, so connecting through them lands every part where
# that file puts it: exported and compared solid by solid, the two agree to
# 4e-10 mm once one difference is taken out, and that difference is the whole
# assembly moved by (-76.862940, -86.199988, 80.297688) mm - the location that
# file gives the plate. An assembly that connects everything has its anchor at
# the origin, because there is nothing left to say where the anchor is, and
# where a product sits in space is not a property of the product.
#
# No two of the eight parts share any volume: the worst of the 28 pairs is
# 0.000000000 mm^3. That is the same check 'AeroAssembly_corrected' passes, and
# here it is a failure rather than a note, because this one is going to be made.
#
# See 'docs.intro' in ../partcad.yaml for what the three stages are for, and
# which interfaces these joints use and why.
name: AeroAssembly_connected
description: >-
  The imported AeroAssembly, corrected and then joined: seven milled parts and
  a bent cap, six M3 bolted joints and a seat, in the order somebody would put
  them together.
links:
  # The fixture. Nothing is connected to it from underneath, so it is what the
  # other seven hang from, and it is placed by being first rather than by
  # saying where it is.
  - part: AeroAssembly_assy_example/AeroFrame_Plate
    name: AeroFrame_Plate
    description: >-
      The plate: two flanges, eight Ø3 clearance holes, and the top face the cap
      stands on.

  # --- the first frame half ------------------------------------------------
  #
  # A half is three parts: a front foot under the plate's front flange, a strut
  # bolted on top of it, and a rear foot bolted under the far end of the strut.
  # It has to go together in that order, because each of the three is located by
  # the one before it.
  - part: AeroAssembly_assy_example/AeroFrameAssembly_solid2
    name: AeroFrameAssembly_solid2
    description: The front foot of the first frame half
    connect:
      withInstance: plate-a
      name: AeroFrame_Plate
      to: aero-plate-opening
      toInstance: frame-front-a
      comment: |
        Offer the foot up under the plate's front flange and start one bolt -
        the 'a' hole of the pair - before the other, so the foot can still swing
        onto the second. Both holes are ISO 273 medium clearance for an M3,
        3.4mm, so neither threads anything: what holds the joint is the bolt and
        its nut, which this assembly does not model.
      how:
        stage: frame-half-1
        pushForceMax: 20.0
        holdWith: aero-foot-opening
        holdWithInstance: strut-a
        holdWithForceMin: 5.0
        holdWithForceMax: 15.0

  - part: AeroAssembly_assy_example/AeroFrameAssembly_solid3
    name: AeroFrameAssembly_solid3
    description: The strut of the first frame half
    connect:
      withInstance: foot-a
      withParams:
        # The strut's flange is not flush with the face it seats on: it stands
        # 14mm above it, and the bolt spans the channel between the two. That
        # is what 'aero-strut-opening' widens 'moveZ' for, and it is stated
        # here because it is a fact about this joint rather than about M3.
        moveZ: 14
      name: AeroFrameAssembly_solid2
      toInstance: strut-a
      comment: |
        The strut seats flat on the foot and is bolted to it through the flange
        14mm above that seat, so the bolt is long enough to cross the channel.
        Start both bolts before tightening either: the strut is what squares
        the half up, and it can only do that while it is free to move.
      how:
        stage: frame-half-1
        pushForceMax: 15.0

  - part: AeroAssembly_assy_example/AeroFrameAssembly_solid1
    name: AeroFrameAssembly_solid1
    description: The rear foot of the first frame half
    connect:
      withInstance: strut-a
      name: AeroFrameAssembly_solid3
      toInstance: rear-a
      toParams:
        # The same 14mm channel at the far end of the strut.
        moveZ: 14
      comment: |
        The rear foot goes on the far end of the strut, and the two bolts that
        hold it are the last of this half. It then bears on the underside of the
        plate's rear flange - bears, and nothing more: the flange's four Ø3
        holes are 2.5mm away from this foot's two, in the source file and
        therefore in all three stages, so there is nothing to bolt there. Both
        patterns are declared on the parts; neither is a joint.
      how:
        stage: frame-half-1
        pushForceMax: 15.0

  # --- the mirrored half ---------------------------------------------------
  #
  # The same three steps on the other side of the plate. This is the half the
  # import put in the wrong place and stage 2 moved; here it is put where its
  # own bolt pattern puts it, and there is no vector to get right.
  - part: AeroAssembly_assy_example/MirrorAeroFrameAssembly_solid2
    name: MirrorAeroFrameAssembly_solid2
    description: The front foot of the mirrored frame half
    connect:
      withInstance: plate-a
      name: AeroFrame_Plate
      to: aero-plate-opening
      toInstance: mirror-front-a
      comment: |
        The other pair of holes in the same flange. In the import this foot was
        (7.728713, -3.380867, 2.583602) mm away from them and its own half was
        2.583602 mm clear of the flange at one end and 12.997 mm^3 inside the
        plate at the other. Connected rather than placed, it cannot be: the
        holes are where it goes.
      how:
        stage: frame-half-2
        pushForceMax: 20.0
        holdWith: aero-foot-opening
        holdWithInstance: strut-a
        holdWithForceMin: 5.0
        holdWithForceMax: 15.0

  - part: AeroAssembly_assy_example/MirrorAeroFrameAssembly_solid3
    name: MirrorAeroFrameAssembly_solid3
    description: The strut of the mirrored frame half
    connect:
      withInstance: foot-a
      withParams:
        moveZ: 14
      name: MirrorAeroFrameAssembly_solid2
      toInstance: strut-a
      how:
        stage: frame-half-2
        pushForceMax: 15.0

  - part: AeroAssembly_assy_example/MirrorAeroFrameAssembly_solid1
    name: MirrorAeroFrameAssembly_solid1
    description: The rear foot of the mirrored frame half
    connect:
      withInstance: strut-a
      name: MirrorAeroFrameAssembly_solid3
      toInstance: rear-a
      toParams:
        moveZ: 14
      comment: |
        The mirror image of the first half's rear foot, down to its two holes
        being 2.5mm off the flange's four - the other way, which is what says
        the mismatch is in the design rather than in one instance of it.
      how:
        stage: frame-half-2
        pushForceMax: 15.0

  # --- the cap -------------------------------------------------------------
  - part: AeroAssembly_assy_example/AeroFrame_Cap
    name: AeroFrame_Cap
    description: The cap, which stands on the plate
    connect:
      withInstance: plate-a
      name: AeroFrame_Plate
      to: aero-cap-seat
      toInstance: cap-a
      comment: |
        The cap is not fastened: it stands on the plate on four feet, and only
        two of them touch. Its front feet are modelled 0.751923 mm too long for
        the faces they land on - the defect stage 1 reports as 90.231 mm^3 of
        metal inside metal - so what is stated here is the pair that does seat,
        and the rear pair stands 0.751923 mm clear. Shim it there; nothing
        fastens through it.
      how:
        stage: cap
        # It only has to be set down.
        pushForceMax: 10.0
